KL DIVERGENCE  (asymmetric / directed)

Notation
  P, Q          discrete probability mass functions  (∑ P = 1, ∑ Q = 1)
  x             outcome / support point
  ln            natural logarithm
  D_KL(P ∥ Q)   expected extra nats when coding samples from P
                using a code that assumes Q

Definition (discrete)
  D_KL(P ∥ Q)  =  ∑_x  P(x) · ln( P(x) / Q(x) )

  (continuous analogue:  ∫ p(x) ln( p(x)/q(x) ) dx )

Two orientations used as variational objectives

1. Exclusive / mode-seeking
     minimise   D_KL(Q ∥ P)  =  ∑ Q(x) ln( Q(x)/P(x) )
     Q is forced to put mass only where P already has mass.
     Extra mass that Q places in P’s “silent” regions is heavily
     penalised → Q collapses onto a single peak of P
     and ignores the remaining modes.

        density
          |     /\
          |    /  \          Q  (tall, narrow)
          |   /    \
          |  /      \   /\
          | /        \/  \     P  (two modes)
          +----------------→  x

2. Inclusive / mass-covering
     minimise   D_KL(P ∥ Q)  =  ∑ P(x) ln( P(x)/Q(x) )
     Every region that carries P-mass must be covered by Q,
     otherwise the log-ratio blows up.
     Q therefore stretches across all modes of P
     (and the empty space between them).

        density
          |  /\          /\
          | /  \        /  \     P
          |/    \______/    \
          |      ________     Q  (flat, wide)
          +----------------→  x

History
  Kullback & Leibler introduced the directed quantity in 1951.
  The two orientations later acquired the names
  “exclusive approximation” and “inclusive approximation”
  in the variational-inference literature.

Properties that follow immediately
  • D_KL(P ∥ Q) ≥ 0   with equality iff P = Q  (Gibbs inequality)
  • not symmetric:    D_KL(P ∥ Q) ≠ D_KL(Q ∥ P) in general
  • not a metric
